{
  /*
   * TypeScript that Node runs directly.
   *
   * There is no build step in this project and adding one would have cost more
   * than the types are worth. From Node 24 the runtime strips type annotations
   * at load, so `node src/index.ts` is the whole story — no transpiler, no
   * output directory, nothing generated that can go stale against its source.
   *
   * Three options make that arrangement honest rather than lucky:
   *
   *   erasableSyntaxOnly          rejects anything Node cannot strip — enums,
   *                               parameter properties, namespaces. Without it
   *                               the types check and the program does not run,
   *                               which is the worst of both.
   *   allowImportingTsExtensions  because the imports name the file that is
   *                               actually there. Node resolves the specifier
   *                               literally; writing `.js` for a file called
   *                               `.ts` is a convention that needs a compiler
   *                               to make true, and there isn't one.
   *   noEmit                      nothing is produced. `tsc` is a checker here,
   *                               not a build.
   *
   * `dom` is in `lib` because the checks under `tools/` drive a real browser,
   * and the callbacks they hand to `page.evaluate` run inside the page --
   * `document` there is not a mistake. Nothing under `src/` touches the DOM.
   *
   * `strict` and no further. `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` was on for one pass
   * and produced thirty complaints about `rows[0]` immediately after a check
   * on `rows.length`, each of which wanted a cast that said nothing. Strict
   * is the bar; stricter than useful is a setting somebody switches off.
   *
   * `src/` carries no explicit `any` at all. The two dozen under `tools/` are
   * the harnesses: a Playwright page handle, and JSON the service returns whose
   * shape belongs to the service rather than to the check. Typing those would
   * mean a second copy of every response shape, kept in step by hand -- which
   * is exactly the sort of duplication the rest of this repository refuses.
   */
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "es2023",
    "lib": ["es2023", "dom"],
    "module": "nodenext",
    "moduleResolution": "nodenext",

    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "erasableSyntaxOnly": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "noEmit": true,

    "strict": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,

    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "types": ["node"]
  },

  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "tools/**/*.ts", "test/**/*.ts"]
}
